 "domain": "Pure Mathematics",
 "standard_code": "A level Mathematics: Pure mathematics",
 "standard_url": "https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/gce-as-and-a-level-mathematics",
 "standard_text": "Differentiate eᵏˣ, aᵏˣ, sin kx, cos kx, tan kx and ln x; use the product rule, the quotient rule and the chain rule; differentiate simple functions defined implicitly or parametrically.",
 "meta_desc": "Free Year 13 A level maths worksheets on differentiation techniques: the chain rule, ln and eˣ, implicit differentiation, parametric differentiation and maximum values. 100 questions with answers.",
 "key_big": "Chain rule: dy/dx = dy/du × du/dx.",
 "key_sub": "Parametric: dy/dx = (dy/dt) ÷ (dx/dt).",
 "body_html": "<h2>How it works</h2><p>Chain rule: for <i>y</i> = f(g(<i>x</i>)), d<i>y</i>/d<i>x</i> = f′(g(<i>x</i>)) × g′(<i>x</i>). d/d<i>x</i>(ln <i>x</i>) = 1/<i>x</i>, d/d<i>x</i>(e<sup><i>kx</i></sup>) = <i>k</i>e<sup><i>kx</i></sup>.</p><p>Implicit: differentiate each term with respect to <i>x</i>; d/d<i>x</i>(<i>y</i>²) = 2<i>y</i> d<i>y</i>/d<i>x</i>. Parametric: d<i>y</i>/d<i>x</i> = (d<i>y</i>/d<i>t</i>) ÷ (d<i>x</i>/d<i>t</i>).</p><div class='sn-ex'><span class='sn-ex-t'>Example</span><p class='sn-ex-q'><i>y</i> = (3<i>x</i> + 1)⁴</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>4(3<i>x</i> + 1)³ × 3</p><p class='sn-ex-a'>Answer: 12(3<i>x</i> + 1)³</p></div><h2>Common mistakes</h2><ul><li>Forgetting to multiply by the derivative of the inside.</li><li>Forgetting dy/dx when differentiating y terms.</li><li>Dividing dx/dt by dy/dt.</li></ul>",
 "faq": [
  {
   "q": "What is the chain rule?",
   "a": "To differentiate a function of a function, differentiate the outside, keep the inside, then multiply by the derivative of the inside."
  },
  {
   "q": "How do you differentiate implicitly?",
   "a": "Differentiate every term with respect to x. Each y term gains a factor dy/dx. Then rearrange for dy/dx."
  }
 ],
